<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nouméa Accord</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1998">1998</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/noumea">Nouméa</a> Accord, signed on 5 May 1998 by the French state and New Caledonia's independence and loyalist movements, set out a gradual transfer of powers to the territory over twenty years. It recognised Kanak identity, created a New Caledonian citizenship and provided for up to three referendums on independence, held in 2018, 2020 and 2021, each of which rejected independence.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The accord, named after New Caledonia's capital and largest city, was the second agreement on the territory's future, after the Matignon Agreements of 1988. For the French Republic it was signed by Lionel Jospin, the prime minister, and Jean-Jack Queyranne, secretary of state for overseas affairs. Jacques Lafleur, Pierre Frogier, Simon Loueckhote, Harold Martin, Jean Lèques and Bernard Deladrière signed for the anti-independence Rally for Caledonia in the Republic (RPCR), and Rock Wamytan, Paul Néaoutyine, Charles Pidjot and Victor Tutugoro for the pro-independence Kanak and Socialist National Liberation Front (FLNKS).</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The accord promised New Caledonia and its indigenous people, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanak-people">Kanaks</a>, greater political power over a transition of twenty years, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> kept control of the military, foreign policy, immigration, police and currency. If the presidency of New Caledonia is held by an opponent of independence, the vice-president must be a supporter of it. Later referendums were to decide whether the territory would remain a special collectivity of France or become an independent state, the last of them to be called by a vote of the Congress of New Caledonia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The accord was put to a popular consultation in New Caledonia on 8 November 1998, and 72% voted in favour. Both signatory parties campaigned for it, the RPCR promising &quot;at least 20 years of peace and stability&quot; and a &quot;future in the Republic&quot;, together with the Socialist Kanak Liberation Party of Nidoïsh Naisseline and the Federation of Committees Coordinating Independents. Against it stood the dissident anti-independence party Une Nouvelle-Calédonie pour tous, which saw it opening the way to a &quot;banana republic&quot;, Guy George's local section of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-front-czechoslovakia">National Front</a>, Claude Sarran's Movement for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and the union USTKE of Louis Kotra Uregi, which wanted immediate independence.</p><p>In the referendums that followed, in 2018 and 2020, majorities chose to remain French. The third, held in December 2021 after a vote of the Congress, widely rejected independence amid a boycott by the independence movement. The accord thus ran its full course of three votes, each of which kept New Caledonia within the French Republic.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nouméa Accord” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Violent conflict in the 1980s ended with the Matignon Accords of 1988 and the <b>Nouméa Accord</b> of 1998.</blockquote><a href="/state/new-caledonia">New Caledonia</a></li></ul></section>
